    "q-254607": {
      "dom_id": "step-q254607",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Emails arrive in a professor's inbox continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ emails per day. If the professor has not received an email for the past $3$ days, what is the probability that an email will arrive in the next $2$ days? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Since emails arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next email using the exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ represent the time, in days, until the next email arrives. Given that emails arrive at an average rate of $2$ per day, the rate parameter is $\\lambda =2$ emails per day, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2,$ $s=3,$ and $t=2.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3+2\\,|\\,X>3) & =P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(2)(2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈0.982.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
